          <span class="pill"><span class="k">base</span> Vacoas, Mauritius (UTC+4)</span>
          <a href="mailto:aminaliali8@gmail.com"><span class="k">mail</span> aminaliali8@gmail.com</a>
          <a href="tel:+23057358608"><span class="k">tel</span> +230 5735 8608</a>
          <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/aminaliali" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="k">in</span> /in/aminaliali</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>What I work on</h2>
      <span class="count">seven practice areas</span>
    </div>
    <div class="caps">
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Internal developer platforms</h3>
        <p>Self-service environments, golden paths, environment templates and standardized delivery workflows that cut the toil out of shipping.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Cloud &amp; infrastructure</h3>
        <p>AWS, Azure, GCP and DigitalOcean, including hybrid patterns and serverless architectures — Lambda, EventBridge, DynamoDB, S3, CDK/SAM.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Kubernetes &amp; containers</h3>
        <p>Kubernetes (CKA), Helm, Argo CD, Docker. Cluster design for production workloads and for local developer sandboxes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Infrastructure as code</h3>
        <p>Terraform modules built for reuse, environment standardization and policy-aligned provisioning across teams.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>CI/CD &amp; release engineering</h3>
        <p>GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, CircleCI, GitOps and progressive delivery — release paths that stay predictable under change.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>Reliability &amp; governance</h3>
        <p>SLO thinking, incident response, postmortems and runbooks; guardrails and least-privilege defaults baked into the delivery flow.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cap">
        <h3>AI &amp; agentic engineering</h3>
        <p>Coding agents pointed at real codebases, MCP servers and in-IDE assistants, and guardrails scoped to what a change can actually reach rather than to file paths.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>Selected work</h2>
      <span class="count">nine pieces</span>
    </div>

    <div class="work">

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Forward Earth</span>2026</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Serverless workloads in a local cluster</h3>
          <p>A Kubernetes-based sandbox for testing AWS Lambda workloads on a developer machine instead of a shared cloud environment. A known tooling limitation blocked the obvious approach, so the cluster runs nested inside its own container runtime.</p>
          <p>Proved with live event-bus and object-storage integration first, then scaled up to validate a full serverless microservice end to end.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Kubernetes</span><span class="tag">AWS Lambda</span><span class="tag">EventBridge</span><span class="tag">S3</span><span class="tag">Docker</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Forward Earth</span>2026</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Reading a serverless estate</h3>
          <p>Reverse-engineered and documented a large serverless architecture so the platform could keep evolving on evidence rather than folklore. Writing it down surfaced consolidation opportunities that fed straight into roadmap planning.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Serverless architecture</span><span class="tag">Architecture documentation</span><span class="tag">Roadmap input</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Forward Earth</span>2026</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Guardrails shaped like the blast radius</h3>
          <p>Path-based guardrails do not hold across service boundaries. An agent told to stay inside <code>service-a</code> obeys, then relaxes a field on a response and stops emitting an event it judged redundant — <code>service-b</code>'s files are byte-identical and <code>service-b</code> is broken.</p>
          <p>Defined the constraints in the vocabulary the damage actually travels in: contracts, events and the edges between services, rather than directories.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Coding agents</span><span class="tag">Guardrails</span><span class="tag">Service contracts</span><span class="tag">Blast radius</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Forward Earth</span>2026 &middot; in progress</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Two agents, two sources of truth</h3>
          <p>Production intent lives in CDK, the local simulation in SAM and LocalStack. Reconciling them takes two narrow agents rather than one holding both contexts: one reads the CDK, one reads the local stack, and each emits the same event-flow schema of resources, edges, configuration and confidence.</p>
          <p>The diff between those two documents is plain code rather than a language model, and the agents exchange only a closed set of typed questions — which is what gives the loop a termination condition.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Multi-agent</span><span class="tag">AWS CDK</span><span class="tag">LocalStack</span><span class="tag">SAM</span><span class="tag">Schema-first</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Forward Earth</span>2026</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Preview environments for async pipelines</h3>
          <p>Scoped adapters that let complex asynchronous data-processing flows run in preview and staging. Most of the work was tracing production behavior across several codebases, so the design fits the orchestration logic already in place rather than replacing it.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Preview environments</span><span class="tag">Async data processing</span><span class="tag">Orchestration</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Bunnyshell</span>2021 &ndash; 2026</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Paved roads across four clouds</h3>
          <p>Terraform modules, Helm charts, Kubernetes manifests and Docker baselines pulled into one reusable delivery standard, with CI/CD running on GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, CircleCI and Argo CD.</p>
          <p>Environment setup and manual release effort each dropped by an estimated 10&ndash;20%, and standardized runbooks made production operations across AWS, Azure, GCP and DigitalOcean repeatable rather than heroic.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Terraform</span><span class="tag">Helm</span><span class="tag">Argo CD</span><span class="tag">Multi-cloud</span><span class="tag">GitOps</span><span class="tag">SRE</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Bunnyshell</span>2024 &ndash; 2025</div>
        <div>
          <h3>AI workloads on the platform</h3>
          <p>Extended the platform to LLM application stacks with ready-made environment templates for LangChain and LlamaIndex, so those stacks could be stood up reproducibly instead of assembled by hand each time.</p>
          <p>Added an MCP server and an in-IDE assistant, putting the platform within reach of the tools engineers already had open.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">LangChain</span><span class="tag">LlamaIndex</span><span class="tag">MCP</span><span class="tag">VS Code extension</span><span class="tag">TypeScript</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">Bunnyshell</span>2021 &ndash; 2026</div>
        <div>
          <h3>Dockerfiles written by reading the repo</h3>
          <p>Contributed to an agentic system that reads a repository — language, package manager, build steps, entrypoint — and writes its Dockerfile, so a service can be containerized without one being hand-authored first.</p>
          <p>Shaped the approach and validated the generated output against real project structures rather than clean examples, which is where generated build files tend to fail.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Agentic AI</span><span class="tag">Code analysis</span><span class="tag">Docker</span><span class="tag">Containerization</span><span class="tag">Developer environments</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="piece">
        <div class="where"><span class="org">edirect</span>2020 &ndash; 2021</div>
        <div>
          <h3>600+ sites, moved in waves</h3>
          <p>Migrated more than 600 websites across providers with phased cutovers, each wave gated by a runbook and validation checkpoints. Cutover-related incidents fell an estimated 10&ndash;15%; a parallel optimization pass took an estimated 8&ndash;15% off cloud spend without trading away reliability.</p>
          <div class="tags"><span class="tag">Migration at scale</span><span class="tag">Phased cutover</span><span class="tag">Cost optimization</span><span class="tag">Runbooks</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

